  Actually, I read a paper a few weeks ago about a parallelized
version of quicksort that was supposed to have big performance gains.
I'm not a CS major, but it seems that your above statement is untrue.

   Consider a mergesort which is about O(n lg n) (can't recall
exactly. It takes about lg n split operations, and ~n operations for the merge
for two sublists). Sort a list of N=65536 elements. Let each split/comparison
operation require 1 cpu tick (unit of time). That's 1048576 operations,
and t~=1048576 ticks to sort the list. But now, split the main list
into two sublists, N_1=32768, N_2=32768 and have two subprocessors
sort them. Each processor requires t_1=t_2~=491520 ticks, but they can
sort the two sublists simultaneously. All that is left is for the main
processor to merge the two lists which is ~O(N), with n=32768.
So the final running time for a two processor mergesort is t~=491520+32768
which is 524288 ticks which is 1048576/2.
(Of course a split and a comparison operation will not require equal time.
 A split can be done with ~N/2 operations, but let's assume we have
a special processor with a split instruction and it takes one cycle.
Also, let's assume that the list elements are strings with fixed length)

  Can anyone look this up in Knuth to confirm it? I'm looking at a merge
routine (linked list) right now that requires 3 comparisons + 3 moves per loop,
plus a constant 2 comparisons (worst case) and 1 move. This comes out
to 6N + 3 operations to merge sublists of size N. That's as far as I can
take the analysis with my limited understanding of computational complexity,
but it seems obvious by inspection that a merge is linear, I don't see
how it can be quadratic. Of course, I have an excuse for any errors
