Date: Thu, 15 Apr 93 10:25:29 CDT
From: eder@hsvaic.boeing.com (Dani Eder)
Subject: Singularity Studies

This is my mechanistic insight.  I assume that 'progress' is measured
by the growth of human population - technology allows more people
to live.  I assume that 'progress' is proportional to human population.
What this means is that the rate of invention is constant per person,
so that if there are more people, inventions are made faster in
a linear relationship.

If I make these assumptions, then the population trends I observe
are the ones I would expect - the population growth rate increases
with population, and hence the time constant for a set increase goes
down.  I would expect 1/population to produce a straight line.

The interruptions I see, such as the Black Death, are caused by
disease, i.e. not affected by the rate of invention.  Once the
plague stopped recurring, the population trends resumed their 
historical pattern.

Going back as far as 10,000 years, the big break in slope seems
to be at the Neolithic Revolution, with the introduction of agriculture
and cities.  There are other changes in slope which I cannot explain
fully now.  I speculate that the slowdown around 3-5,000 years ago
was from the big invention of agriculture having mostly run it's course,
but the next big invention or inventions not getting going for a
while.

For reference, the population of the world 12,000 years ago is
estimated at only 4 million.  If we divide the 1.2 trillion
person-years of mankind from then through the year 2000, the
quintile points are 200AD, 1230AD, 1750AD, and 1940AD.  In other
words, equal amounts of mankinds existence occurs between these
point when measured in total person-years.

Another way to break history down is into thirds, which I will
call Ancient, Medieval, and Modern.  The breakpoints are 960 AD
and 1840 AD.

As far as whether there is a reasonable mechanism to have an
infinite population, I can show you that space colonies could
support an ordinary human population of 10^15 in this solar
system alone.  Then you get into machine life, and the upper
bound gets very high.  

My intent was to show another indicator of when the Singualrity
might occur, this one based on history.  I don't think that
population will truly go to infinity, but a population of
10^20 based on machine life would be hard to tell from infinity
from our point of view.

Dani Eder

