Date: Thu, 15 Apr 93 13:42:11 -0400
From: pavel@PARK.BU.EDU (Paul Cisek)
Subject: TECH: Genetic algorithms question

I have some questions for the Genetic Algorithms people out there.  I hope
they're of interest to the list...

In biological genetics, there is often a relationship between genes within a
pair of alleles where one is dominant over the other.  A recessive genes is
consequently expressed much less often than the dominant one.  My first
question is: What determines the dominance of one gene over another?  Is it a
part of the code in the cistron itself or is it a property of the chromosome
that it happens to be carried by?  The simple example I always hear is that
blue eye color is recessive and brown is dominant.  I'm sure most traits,
including eye color are actually much more complicated than that, but
nevertheless, is the following reasoning accurate:

Whenever there exists a relationship between genes where one is dominant over
another, the recessive one is expressed less often.  Thus, it is not
selected-against in the population as severely as the dominant (even a very
damaging gene can survive in organisms where it's masked by a dominant one).
Thus, we can expect a pool of recessive genes to show more variety in
mutations than the dominant ones do.  When a population decreases in size, the
likelihood that an organism winds up with two recessive genes increases, and
thus we expect more mutations (as seen in cases of inbreeding).
We seem to wind up with an automatic mechanism for increasing mutation rate
during times of evolutionary stress upon a population (a kind of emergency
system).

Is this right?  Please point out any problems with any of the above steps.
(BTW - I've read the Selfish Gene, and am not arguing for selection
among populations or even individuals, but just pointing out an effect that
selection among genes can have on the evolutionary rate in a population).  My
next question is:  Has this kind of mechanism been attempted in genetic
algorithms?  What kind of results were observed?

-Paul


