I believe that there is strong, albeit inconclusive, evidence that  
P-complete problems do not parallelize as much as one would like: As  
I understand it, no one has found any P-complete problem in NC---the  
set of problems that can be solved in poly-logarithmic time, using a  
polynomial number of processors.  An equivalent view of NC is the set  
of problems that can be computed by a circuit of poly-log depth, and  
of polynomial size. (BTW, using a circuit of exponential size  
probably would not help for physical reasons.)  P-complete problems,  
by definition, have a worst-case complexity that is polynomial.  I  
seem to recall (don't hold me to this) that, for example, MAXIMAL  
CLIQUE (i.e., a set of nodes that form a clique, but which are NOT  
part of a bigger clique) is P-complete.

I am not a theoretician, so I am not well-versed in these matters.   
One, of course, should be careful about drawing conclusions about  
theoretical evidence without knowledge of the details.  Sometimes,  
theoretical complexity results are of little practical consequence.

