From: hal@alumni.cco.caltech.edu (Hal Finney)
Subject: Singularity Studies

Responding to Dani's prediction of a population singularity:

Remember Nick's point about the S-shaped growth curve (often called the
"yeast growth curve").  At the left part, it looks exponential.  A little
yeast cell extrapolating at that time would predict huge growth rates.
But that extrapolation is meaningless.  You can't tell an S-shaped curve
from an exponential one at first.  So we really have no basis for concluding
that population, or computer speed, will continue to increase exponentially.

Many industrial technologies have gone through S-shaped curves.  Locomotive
speeds; cotton production as the cotton gin was being refined; etc.  At one
time such mundane businesses as textiles were exciting, fast-moving fields
where each year new techniques and processes were obsoleting the old.  There
is no reason to expect that the "hot" fields of today will be immune.

Hal

