From: hal@alumni.cco.caltech.edu (Hal Finney)
Subject: COMP: Parallel processing

My experience is that Eric is right that for any given problem of a specified
size, there is a limit to how much parallel computing power you can throw
at it.  This observation has taken on the status of a "law" (I forget the
name) in the parallel computing field.

BUT - there is a countervailing factor, which is that if you scale up problem
size as you scale up the number of processors, you can retain nearly 100%
efficiency.  And this is in practice what is done.  People don't use
thousand-processor systems to solve the little problems that bogged down their
uniprocessors.  Instead they run great big problems on these machines,
bogging them down just as thoroughly and justifying expenditures on even larger
designs.

Hal

