From: jrk@information-systems.east-anglia.ac.uk (Richard Kennaway)
Subject: Complexity of sorting (was Re: Extropian Priorities)

Ray Cromwell writes:
>  I'm rather confused about this whole sorting business. I thought it was
>generally conceded that no sort could be better than O(n*log(n)) on
>average, but apparently there are two algorithms out there which claim
>they can beat this.  The compact radix sort (based on radix tree) was
>recently posted to comp.theory and the author claims it is O(N).
>(surprisingly, I didn't see anyone successfully refute it). Also,
>a year ago, I heard about some new sort called the Fusion sort based on
>'fusion trees' which supposedly beat n*log(n).

I haven't seen the posts on comp.theory, but I'll take a look.  I haven't
heard of fusion trees.

There are two arguments for the O(n*log(n)) lower bound, one restricted but
precise, one general but dubious.

The restricted, precise version is that if a sorting algorithm proceeds by
performing pairwise comparisons of elements of the list, then it must
require at least O(n*log(n)) comparisons on average.  This is because each
comparison gives 1 bit of information, there are n! possible outcomes for
the sort, and log(n!) = O(n*log(n)).

Note that this argument says nothing about the possibility of doing better
with parallelism.  It's a bound on the total work to be done, not the time
taken to do it.  I can immediately think of at least one way to sort in
O(n) time using comparisons and O(n) parallelism (ignoring the costs of
communicating among such a huge number of parallel processes).

Radix sort does not work by pairwise comparisons, so this argument is not
applicable to it.

The general but dubious derivation of O(n*log(n)) is on information-
theoretic grounds.  As mentioned above, any sorting algorithm must somehow
generate O(n*log(n)) bits of information to sort an arbitrary list of n
elements.  Supposedly this must take a proportional amount of time.  A
rigorous argument to this effect appears wanting.  I might believe
O(log(n*log(n))) time to generate n bits, but that's a lot smaller -- in
fact, it's overwhelmed by the O(n) time required just to read and write the
whole list.

In practice, when keys are short, radixsort is linear for all practical
purposes, and the information-theoretic argument can be ignored.

In general, the proper choice of sorting algorithm depends on the
particular problem.  All have their own strengths and weaknesses, and
running time is only one of several properties which will determine the
choice.  Radix sort is very fast, but can require a lot of temporary
workspace.

Extropian relevance?  Hmm, what about that information-theoretic argument? 
Another "fundamental limit" that isn't, like the kT limit that can be
circumvented by reversible computation and the CV limit that several people
have recently found a way around?  I remember an article in Sci.Am. many
years ago (no reference) about completely different types of machine for
solving problems such as sorting much faster.  For example, how long does
it take to find the maximum element of a list?  O(n), surely, since you
must at least look at each one?  Not if the elements are pieces of uncooked
spaghetti of various lengths.  To find the longest, stand them upright in a
bundle on a flat surface, then the longest can be picked out easily in O(1)
time.

