I like Nick's idea of an investment firm which goes for really low-probability
but high-payoff investments, and diversifies to protect itself from risk.
I have seen occasional claims in the literature that high-risk returns are
higher than they should be in a fully rational market.  This whole approach
reminds me of Heinlein's "Long Range Foundation".

But, the big problem is in estimating the probability of success.  Where do we
get a number like 1% for the chance of coming up with a smart drug that
raises IQ to 150?  You can't just go on "gut feel", not if you're investing
other people's money.  But there is so little information available as to
whether success is possible.

There is a lot of philosophical literature about estimating probabilities in
states of near-zero information.  A classic example was "is there life on
Mars", asked at a time when we knew virtually nothing about that planet.
No agreement seems to exist about what methodology is optimal to use for
problems of this type.  I'd like to see more work in this area.  We had
something here a while back about a class of problems for which very little
information was given and there was a provably optimal strategy involving
the constant 1/e.  I wonder if something like this could be applied to
uncertain problems like those Nick describes.

