>if the elements are pieces of uncooked spaghetti of various lengths.   
>To find the longest, stand them upright in a bundle on a flat  
>surface, then the longest can be picked out easily in O(1) time.

I believe that the time, using this approach, can be bounded from  
below by Omega (sqrt(n)).  Assume that the speed of light is fixed.  
The time needed to detect the longest strand of spaghetti, then is  
bounded from below by the time for light to travel the distance from  
the longest strand to they eye of the observer.  According to the way  
you have described the algorithm, the strands are held in a bundle (a  
cylinder). Its radius is Omega(sqrt(n)).  


One can arrange things differently, but I doubt that a time of O(1)  
can be achieved for any finite n.

BTW, sorting does parallelize nicely.  Batcher gave a O(log^2(n))  
algorithm many years ago that helped to popularize the  
shuffle-exchange network.

