 My assertion implicitly included the claim that typical problems which will
require faster processors can benefit significantly from parallelization.
I stand by this claim, although I don't claim that the speedup will be
linear in the number of processors, nor do I mean to trivialize the paradigm
shift that will be needed.

 Sorting n numbers on a array of n processors has been proven to require
O(sqrt(n)) time, due to the time required to move the data from one end of
the array to the other. (I am ignoring the better than O(log^2 n) time
achievable on hypercube-like networks because the hardware doesn't scale up
well enough due to fan-out and/or wire length problems).
 I think this is sufficient to demonstrate that the limits on single processor
speeds won't be a major deterent to the singularity.
