From: kqb@whscad1.att.com
Subject: COMP: Complexity of sorting

Ray Cromwell writes:
>  I'm rather confused about this whole sorting business. I thought it was
>generally conceded that no sort could be better than O(n*log(n)) on
>average, but apparently there are two algorithms out there which claim
>they can beat this.  ...

Complexity bounds depend on the model of computation chosen.
If I recall correctly from 15 years ago, the O(n*log(n)) lower
bound for sorting assumes that the comparison tree uses only analytic
functions.  A floor function is not analytic, though, and can be used
for a faster sorting algorithm, or at least a faster *expected
time" sorting algorithm.  (I'll skip radix sort since someone
already mentioned that.  Also, I'll assume only one processor
is used to do the comparisons.)

Bruce Weide's 1978 Carnegie-Mellon Computer Science Department
thesis ("Statistical Methods in Algorithm Design and Analysis")
describes a simple and fairly general-purpose linear expected
time sorting algorithm using the floor function.  It is easiest
to understand if the N numbers to be sorted come from a uniform
distribution over the interval [0,N), but most any ordinary kind of
distribution is OK because in sublinear time one can sample some
of the N numbers and construct a function that will transform
(in linear time) the N numbers so that they are approximately
uniformly distributed.

To sort N uniformly distributed numbers in linear expected time,
do the following:

  (1) create an array of N/C bins, for some constant C
  (2) create an (initially empty) linked list for each of
      those N/C bins,
  (3) for each number n
        add n to the linked list for bin |_ n/C _|
  (4) for each of the N/C linked lists
        sort the list in constant expected time
        output the sorted list

For the full details, read Bruce Weide's thesis or some reference to it.

